    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Tarok Slovenian',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Slovenian Tarok is the most popular parlour game in Slovenia, a point trick-taking game with bidding closely related to Austrian Tarock. 54 cards: 8 per suit (Clubs, Spades, Hearts, Diamonds) plus 22 trumps (taroks). Highest trump Å kis, then Mond (XXI), lowest trump (I). Black suits K-Q-Knight-J-10-9-8-7; red K-Q-Knight-J-1-2-3-4. Kings, Å kis, Mond, lowest trump 5 pts each; Queens 4, Knights 3, Jacks 2, others 1. Four players; dealer gives 12 cards each in two batches of 6 (counter-clockwise), 6 in talon. Bidding from Three up to Valat Without. With three/two/one declarer calls a King for secret partner. Declarer's side wins by taking more than half the card points.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Most popular parlour game in Slovenia; closely related to Austrian KÃ¶nigrufen. 54-card pack, 4 players, bidding and calling a King; declarer's side aims for majority of card points.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Four players. The four-player version is the most popular form for social play; one player is dealer, and partnerships are determined by bidding and by calling a King in three, two, or one contracts.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '54 cards (22 taroks + 8 per suit)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '54-card Tarock pack, score sheet',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "First 6 cards to talon; then 12 cards each in two batches of 6, counter-clockwise starting to dealer's right.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Calling a king or trula: If declarer holds all four kings in three/two/one, some allow calling a trula card instead; if holding all kings and all trula, some allow calling a trump.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Piccolo: Optional negative contract: win exactly one trick. Worth 70. Ranks between solo one and beggar. Not all circles allow it.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: "Slovenian Tarok is the most popular parlour game in Slovenia, a point trick-taking game with bidding closely related to Austrian Tarock. 54 cards: 8 per suit (Clubs, Spades, Hearts, Diamonds) plus 22 trumps (taroks). Highest trump Å kis, then Mond (XXI), lowest trump (I). Black suits K-Q-Knight-J-10-9-8-7; red K-Q-Knight-J-1-2-3-4. Kings, Å kis, Mond, lowest trump 5 pts each; Queens 4, Knights 3, Jacks 2, others 1. Four players; dealer gives 12 cards each in two batches of 6 (counter-clockwise), 6 in talon. Bidding from Three up to Valat Without. With three/two/one declarer calls a King for secret partner. Declarer's side wins by taking more than half the card points.",
